From e486b5b14e0ed22816e7374288c8243239c7960d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: edwardzhu02 <explodingtnt@foxmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 15:06:47 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] hp39gii: run KhiCAS (giac) out of the paged window

The calculator has 512 KB of SRAM and the giac engine is 3.2 MB of Thumb
code, so it cannot be linked in.  It is linked into a .pgtext section at
0x00400000 instead, demand-paged one 4 KB page at a time from NAND, and
started from the NSH command "khicas".

Paging layout: the first megabyte stays one identity section (image, heap,
stacks, page tables), and the window is a separate 3.75 MB virtual range
that sits above physical RAM - so nothing that must stay resident can be
evicted by accident.  The window is mapped through four coarse tables at
0x6B000 and backed by 16 frames at 0x6C000.

The pager itself was already in the tree; three things had to change for
real code to survive it:

  - The reverse map (which page a frame holds) is now initialised.  Without
    it the first 16 faults were fine and everything after that executed a
    page that had been handed to someone else.
  - GPMI: when a page is filled from an abort handler, interrupts are
    masked, so none of the three GPMI ISRs run.  Their sticky completion
    latches (APBH CH4 CMDCMPLT, ECC8 COMPLETE) then block the *next* chain:
    it consumes the semaphore, times out, and silently hands back the
    previous page.  GPMI_PolledComplete() clears them after a polled wait.
    Symptom was "every second 2 KB read returns the previous page", i.e. a
    page whose two halves were identical.
  - hp39gii_pagetest: Thumb stubs, because the engine is Thumb code.

The resident half of the application (khicas_app.cpp, khicas_stubs.cpp,
khicas_compat.c) is built into libkhicas.a by scripts/build-khicas.sh: it is
compiled against the toolchains newlib headers rather than NuttXs, which
is the ABI the prebuilt giac archives were built for, and it runs the C++
static constructors itself (CONFIG_HAVE_CXXINITIALIZE would drag in the
NuttX C++ runtime, which is not that ABI).

The engine ships as a file, /mnt/khicas.bin, so it is data and not part of
the kernel image.  The pager cannot read through the file system from an
abort handler, so boot copies the file into a raw staging area at NAND
blocks 800.. when the two differ (nand_install_khicas()).

Also here: the file system partition moves from block 245 to 247 - the
NuttX image grew past two blocks - and CONFIG_STMP3770_KHICAS in Kconfig,
on in the board defconfig.
